  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frost and/or freezing temperatures this \nweekend.\n\nTemperatures drop today in the wake of a strong cold front with\nhighs expected to be in the 50s for most(40s mtns). High\npressure builds overhead throughout today with clearing skies \nand diminishing winds expected. This creates a favorable setup \nfor radiational cooling tonight, with lows dropping into the \nupper 20s to low 40s across the area. A Freeze Warning has been \nissued for those along and west of the Blue Ridge and expanding \ninto portions of NE Maryland. Additionally,a Frost Advisory has \nbeen issued for a areas west of I-95 and east of the Freeze \nWarning. \n\nTemperatures begin gradually warming on Sunday although\nadditional Frost Advisories are possible Sunday night west of\nthe Blue Ridge where low temperatures drop into the 30s.\nBeginning early next week, chances for Frost/freeze diminish as\ntemperatures continue to warm. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily chances for showers next week as a Nor'easter \ndevelops off the Carolina coast.\n\nAs an upper trough advances through the Mid-Atlantic today, part of \nit cuts off along a stalled front south of our region this weekend. \nHeading into next week, this cutoff upper-level low results in the \ndevelopment of a nor'easter off the Carolina coast. This system then \nmeanders offshore and near the coast, but guidance is uncertain on \nits track and strength. Forecast models show considerable spread \nwith respect to the location nor'easter next week. Some solutions \nkeep the system offshore, while others have it retrograding further \nwestward. If this system does track further westward, it could lead \nto local impacts. With this mid-level low being cut off from the \nmain steering flow, it could linger through much of next week. \nNortheast breezes and daily chances for showers are possible. \nRegions along and east of I-95 have the best potential for showers \nnext week.",
